{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XuCJvao7rxHnU4c8ZEFvqq","type":"tool_result","content":"55\t  return (\n56\t    <button className={`${colors[color]} rounded-full px-2 py-1.5 font-sans text-sm/6 font-medium shadow`}>\n57\t      {children}\n58\t    </button>\n59\t  );\n60\t}\n61\t```\n62\t\n63\tThen it tries to generate the CSS for all of these tokens, throwing away any tokens that don't map to a utility class the framework knows about.\n64\t\n65\t### Dynamic class names\n66\t\n67\tSince Tailwind scans your source files as plain text, it has no way of understanding string concatenation or interpolation in the programming language you're using.\n68\t\n69\t<TipBad>{<>Don't construct class names dynamically</>}</TipBad>\n70\t\n71\t```html\n72\t<!-- [!code filename:HTML] -->\n73\t<div class=\"text-{{ error ? 'red' : 'green' }}-600\"></div>\n74\t```\n75\t\n76\tIn the example above, the strings `text-red-600` and `text-green-600` do not exist, so Tailwind will not generate those classes.\n77\t\n78\tInstead, make sure any class names you’re using exist in full:\n79\t\n80\t<TipGood>{<>Always use complete class names</>}</TipGood>\n81\t\n82\t```html\n83\t<!-- [!code filename:HTML] -->\n84\t<!-- [!code word:text-red-600] -->"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8bcdf181-519e-4dc0-b520-02492e10db3c","uuid":"ee6705ea-3ea0-477a-b3d7-2250ad7d500b","timestamp":"2026-09-23T15:17:43.663Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/jcarr/.cache/markdownattractor/bench/graphify/tailwind-css/src/src/docs/detecting-classes-in-source-files.mdx","content":"  return (\n    <button className={`${colors[color]} rounded-full px-2 py-1.5 font-sans text-sm/6 font-medium shadow`}>\n      {children}\n    </button>\n  );\n}\n```\n\nThen it tries to generate the CSS for all of these tokens, throwing away any tokens that don't map to a utility class the framework knows about.\n\n### Dynamic class names\n\nSince Tailwind scans your source files as plain text, it has no way of understanding string concatenation or interpolation in the programming language you're using.\n\n<TipBad>{<>Don't construct class names dynamically</>}</TipBad>\n\n```html\n<!-- [!code filename:HTML] -->\n<div class=\"text-{{ error ? 'red' : 'green' }}-600\"></div>\n```\n\nIn the example above, the strings `text-red-600` and `text-green-600` do not exist, so Tailwind will not generate those classes.\n\nInstead, make sure any class names you’re using exist in full:\n\n<TipGood>{<>Always use complete class names</>}</TipGood>\n\n```html\n<!-- [!code filename:HTML] -->\n<!-- [!code word:text-red-600] -->","numLines":30,"startLine":55,"totalLines":316}}}
